# Delivers the CONTENTS of a curated directory before a new file is added to it.
#
# The gap: `30-reuse-first` fires on new .tsx under src/*/ui/ and explicitly
# EXCLUDES tests/. `component-reuse-check` reads className markup, so it is .tsx
# only. Between them, creating a new file in a small curated directory — where a
# duplicate is silent, passes, and is maintained forever — is unguarded.
#
# Real instance, 2026-09-08: `tests/sop/counted-claims.test.ts` was written to
# enforce that counted claims in docs match the disk. `claude-md-handbook-agreement`
# already enforced exactly that, including to disk. Nothing fired, and nothing
# could have. The suite was deleted the same session.
#
# WHY THIS PRINTS A LIST RATHER THAN ASKING A QUESTION. Duplicate detection does
# not work: measured recall for independently-written same-behaviour code is under
# 1% (Juergens et al., CSMR 2010), and the benchmark that suggested otherwise is
# 93% mislabelled. So there is no check to run. What HAS been measured to prevent
# a duplicate is delivering the existing candidates at the moment of authoring —
# Ye & Fischer, ICSE 2002: of 20 delivered components the subjects reused, they
# "did not anticipate the existence of 9", which "could not have been reused
# without the support of CodeBroker". Five subjects, never replicated at scale,
# and still the only positive result in the literature. So: no judgement, no
# question, just the list.
#
# Scope is two directories on purpose. Both are small, curated, and carry a
# registry that makes a duplicate expensive: tests/sop/ (46 enforcer suites, whose
# count is pinned in CLAUDE.md and the handbook) and tests/helpers/ (the canonical
# fakes, which `canonical-fakes.test.ts` polices for NEW suites hand-rolling a
# covered fake). Adding a third directory means measuring that it is small enough
# for the listing to be readable, and that duplicates there actually cost something.
#
# Fires only on Write, only for a path that does not exist yet. Editing an existing
# file in these directories is not the reflex being guarded, and the tool layer
# already refuses to edit a file that was not read.
#
# .rule files can only exit 2 (show the message) or 0 (silent) — there is no
# "print and proceed". So this interrupts once per file creation, which is rare by
# construction. If it ever becomes frequent, that is the signal to narrow it, not
# to raise the cap.
rule_id=registry-dir
rule_once=0

rule_match() {
    [ "$1" = "Write" ] || return 1
    [ -n "$3" ] || return 1
    case "$3" in
        */tests/sop/*|*/tests/helpers/*) ;;
        *) return 1 ;;
    esac
    # Exists already → an edit, not a new member.
    [ -e "$3" ] && return 1
    return 0
}

rule_message() {
    # $FILE is what the router parsed from tool_input.file_path; rule_message
    # takes no arguments (see router.sh, `rule_message >&2`).
    dir=$(dirname "$FILE")
    cat <<MSG
You are adding a NEW file to a curated directory. Here is what is already in it.

$(ls -1 "$dir" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/  /')

Read the ones whose name is close to yours BEFORE writing. A second enforcer or a
second fake does not fail anything — it passes, it looks like coverage, and it is
maintained forever. On 2026-09-08 a suite was written here that duplicated
\`claude-md-handbook-agreement.test.ts\` and had to be deleted the same session.

This is a listing, not an accusation. If yours is genuinely different, say how in
its header and carry on.
MSG
}
